#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199370} :activity/id #uuid "9b6bcbf9-9e97-506c-ac92-2753f429a355" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050851 :topic/id #uuid "6bf185a6-38f8-583f-b073-169dac3d7fe6" :topic/math-academy-id 4208} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199234 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198228 :question/id #uuid "5524aeab-cc64-5da8-bea3-8cd5dd86ebcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5264"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050148}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496264}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"} :step/id #uuid "82c5626f-5ffa-5852-9567-16e71d3f88de" :step/math-academy-id 19456 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199371}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199240 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198229 :question/id #uuid "b1f59935-cd61-5c59-aa8d-cf7ba7f151bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5618"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510411}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"} :step/id #uuid "fd96ee0e-953b-5e30-a73d-de454e088881" :step/math-academy-id 20423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199499 :tutorial/content "Now, let's consider the intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the plane \n$z = x+y-\\dfrac{1}{4},$\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d4b8db0985d437807f6e580bc6c2c16cacca1ea7c8e49ed4653717cdb3c9e27.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the intersection of the paraboloid and plane is given by the solution to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ z=x+y-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n- To find the projection of the intersection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z$ from the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/52/520b57cf872ede31d4397d59a833c565147d82a45be1d6b2634f82e1effdff8c.png)\n\n \n\n- To find the projection of the intersection onto the $xz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/05/059acf1ec5dadaa98b2cd4d4b6c79884e4facc96dd49664abbc247cfba86aaeb.png)\n\n \n\n- To find the projection of the intersection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x$ from the system. The resulting projection curve will be an ellipse, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/80/80d3042d21d2e7265a40739ae1531ac30b03fea16fa67609a4e1b0eac8559411.png)\n\n \n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "1d0d1b4d-a008-573c-b368-9ab2bfc7f41c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7833 :tutorial/title "Intersections of Surfaces With Planes"} :step/id #uuid "d692d6c4-d307-5093-8885-932a55dd02f4" :step/math-academy-id 45271 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199240}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199500 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to find the points of intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the line with the equation\n$x = -y = -z+1.$\nThe line, paraboloid, and their points of intersection are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5159ac99a1bc9f73c01df3c2cc2e6fb2093a3d68e00731609d7b62a6fa55149d.png)\n\n \n\nThe trick is to express the equation of the line in parametric form, substitute the parametric equations into the equation of the surface, and solve for the parameter.\n\nSo, first, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n![](images/01/01357a5c6b72d7c976765aeed04db8afa3e3a80e13095d9be1ea2f2594ae5bf5.svg)\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nOur intersection points must belong to the line and the surface simultaneously. So, substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (-t+1) & =(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t^2+t - 1=0.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n$t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nSubstituting these values back into the parametric equations of the line\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-(\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z=-(\\frac{1}{2})+1=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}).$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=-(-1)=1 \\\\ z=-(-1)+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2})$ and $(-1,1,2).$ \n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "90b0a614-08e3-5d8b-bcf4-28aacc99e1ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7834 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "152c742b-df15-5eb5-a164-c09238c5b2e6" :step/math-academy-id 45272 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199234}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199371 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198075 :question/id #uuid "89942534-6b03-52ec-8494-830de5eb8128" :question/math-academy-id "e-12558"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510412}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"} :step/id #uuid "62b86b82-79e2-51cc-abf3-68181195c0c8" :step/math-academy-id 45293 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199369}}] :activity/title "Intersections of Lines and Planes With Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198505}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510550} :activity/id #uuid "6c80042f-f7cd-5e07-8f32-4eebe739d487" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050851 :topic/id #uuid "6bf185a6-38f8-583f-b073-169dac3d7fe6" :topic/math-academy-id 4208} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510479 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510411 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172f9044-b08d-597e-a42f-5e850c62dc68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-8)^{2}}{1/3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509731} {:answer/id #uuid "22ebb37c-8c26-51e9-9c62-0f2d00524ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{2/3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509760} {:answer/id #uuid "78a92225-282a-5fa9-927d-dd473156f003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509945} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a7513d-02fd-5ce8-a27d-be0b1651e1a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510047} {:answer/id #uuid "bbef375e-e5f8-5e13-bf7c-2fbf0dbcd8ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510086}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510047} :answer-field/id #uuid "57bc7b16-076a-5d7f-9700-faa7bbcaa22a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79a93a5e-93ce-572a-a9b2-6cc224e62f04" :question/math-academy-id "q-71144" :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $\\frac{2(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=1$ and the plane $2x-z=8$ onto the $xy$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-z & =8\\,⟹\\,z=2x-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(2x-8)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(2(x-4))^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{4(x-4)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ 2(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "07630e9e-6eaa-5c9c-8cca-3f82529d648d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510523}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023e9917-3ef8-5f06-9f86-33a31fac22e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-11,-5,-7)$ and $(1,1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509700} {:answer/id #uuid "620ccc76-b273-5913-9295-a74568d6b881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509893} {:answer/id #uuid "77a523c2-195a-5b93-b4f7-8f2f2ba7c2a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-3,-1,-3)$ and $(9,5,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509939} {:answer/id #uuid "81c8c0cf-32fa-5ec8-ae30-58c2cefd375c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-7,-3,-5)$ and $(-3,-1,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509974} {:answer/id #uuid "8a82b00a-4358-523b-a223-90c195948106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-11,-5,-7)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509992}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509700} :answer-field/id #uuid "264de4f5-8c74-5910-abc7-434d194ea07f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79e25129-bb03-5263-b94d-378f30c636ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-94778" :question/problem "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=4{z}^{2}$ and the line $\\frac{x+1}{2}=y=z+2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{2}=y=z+2=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t-1 \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=t-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =4{z}^{2} \\\\ (2t-1)^{2}+3{t}^{2} & =4(t-2)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2}-4t+1+3{t}^{2} & =4({t}^{2}-4t+4) \\\\ 4{t}^{2}-4t+1+3{t}^{2} & =4{t}^{2}-16t+16 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}+12t-15 & =0 \\\\ 3({t}^{2}+4t-5) & =0 \\\\ 3(t+5)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-5,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2(-5)-1=-11 \\\\ y=-5 \\\\ z=-5-2=-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-11,-5,-7).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2(1)-1=1 \\\\ y=1 \\\\ z=1-2=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(1,1,-1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-11,-5,-7)$ and $(1,1,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "8cafab22-633e-567a-b060-34f181932e1e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510542 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49d7a3f7-5a7c-529b-8365-112a6e047f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,-1,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509838} {:answer/id #uuid "4b015270-c477-5154-96e4-6cc157ae07cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No points of intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509840} {:answer/id #uuid "58893bd2-e6a6-54b6-a356-759f3915936a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,-2,1)$ and $(-1,-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509872} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ffed4d-2482-5099-8b82-bd20e8d364c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,-1,2)$ and $(-1,-2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510044} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3d4c3e-b241-54c6-acec-d0f88c27e4bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,-1,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510157}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510044} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a536d68-bf2d-5dad-99bc-e23331bfdfe7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2fc9ea0f-3211-5796-852b-f82127f7f8bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-70492" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}+(z-2)^{2}=9$ and the line $x=y+1=\\frac{z-2}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z-2}{2}=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=t-1 \\\\ z=2t+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}+(z-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (t)^{2}+((t-1)+4)^{2}+((2t+2)-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (t)^{2}+(t+3)^{2}+(2t)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {t}^{2}+({t}^{2}+6t+9)+4{t}^{2} & =9 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+6t+9 & =9 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+6t & =0 \\\\ t(t+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(0)=0 \\\\ y=(0)-1=-1 \\\\ z=2(0)+2=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(0,-1,2).$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=(-1)-1=-2 \\\\ z=2(-1)+2=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,-2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(0,-1,2)$ and $(-1,-2,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "cb05386a-05eb-5248-b754-a8d8d5a583d5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510479}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510550 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01030459-2127-5845-a022-909ed458b20a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509696} {:answer/id #uuid "077958e9-019b-52b4-a194-2e3d1e6e8a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509708} {:answer/id #uuid "143f96a1-49b9-5c94-8498-9b59fb558656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509723} {:answer/id #uuid "203cf6b6-970b-533e-8eff-91d74ba85161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509751} {:answer/id #uuid "fb60e82e-e2d6-5601-ad2a-ed678e54b305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510228}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509696} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9278757-a57e-52ac-9515-43ca861ad9fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4aa33ace-7f3a-5239-9095-80fa2dcc03c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35493" :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{3}=-1$\nand the plane $3y+z=-2$  onto the\n$xy$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n3y+z=-2\\,⟹\\,z=-2-3y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the hyperboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{((-2-3y)+2)^{2}}{3} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{3} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2{y}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4{y}^{2} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}=-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "e330baa0-4535-5227-ae2f-5e7087691796" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510565}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510565 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2903bd31-59f2-5edf-ba78-0b700636216f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495599} {:answer/id #uuid "72fcd5f6-74c1-548c-b798-b9f86c8b5a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495763} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e331d6-2b84-5961-a705-7c0440233497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495990} {:answer/id #uuid "e4145407-31af-5f0e-be8b-a08fc7a69e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496016} {:answer/id #uuid "e87b73a0-162f-5a86-8eb0-04ffa6648475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495990} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac07d115-2c5e-5c77-b205-49ff29ccdb65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c16a549-c615-5f5a-9201-b01b9bbc4908" :question/math-academy-id "q-36325" :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet \n$(x+3)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-2{z}^{2}=1$\n and the plane   $x+2z=-3$ onto the\n$xy$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx+2z=-3\\,⟹\\,z=-\\frac{x+3}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the hyperboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-2{(-\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "f9fa29ad-e929-5ec2-a636-229382ae0cca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510542}}] :activity/title "Intersections of Lines and Planes With Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509684}]}
